// winvar.cla: gated UI scenario (clarusc-ui-gaps Task 3b) -- execution-level
// coverage for the window-var construction fix. LogWin's `var log: text` has
// no initializer, and LogWin declares NO `on opened` handler at all (see
// testdata/emitui/opendoc.cla's Reader window for the compile-level model
// of this exact shape), so the only construction of `log`'s rt_text handle
// comes from clarusc's fabricated `opened` glue (lowSynthesizeMissingOpened-
// Handlers), not the explicit-`on opened` path (lowPrependWinVarDefaults)
// hscroll.cla and dialogs.cla used to exercise. Pre-fix, that fabricated
// glue never constructed the handle at all (rt_ui_open zeroes window state
// via NewHandleClear), so Add.click's `log.append(...)` -- an IN-PLACE
// mutation of `log`, not a rebind -- dereferenced a NULL rt_text* and
// crashed (or silently corrupted whatever memory that NULL pointer aliased).
//
// Add.click appends first, then surfaces the accumulated value via
// `Body.text = log`. `title = log` (text -> string(255)) was tried first
// and is NOT legal: `title`'s runtime type is string(255), and Ch3's
// String<->Text coercion runs Str->Text only, not back -- both clarus check
// and clarusc emit reject it with "cannot assign text to string". Note also
// that rt_ui_widget_set_text (textview.text's setter) has no RT_MAC_TEST
// trace line of its own (unlike a Str255 widget-set's `T SET`, see
// rt_ui.c's own comment on rt_ui_widget_get_text/set_text), so the
// accumulated text is verified only by S1's snap, not the trace.
//
// Coordinate math mirrors buttons.cla's convention: the window opens
// centered, left=(512-W)/2 and top=44 for a fitting window. W=300 here, so
// left=106, top=44 -- the same origin buttons.cla's 300-wide Panel opens
// at. Add is at the same local rect buttons.cla's Go button uses, (20,20)-
// (100,40), so a click at local (50,30) = global (106+50, 44+30) = (156,74)
// lands inside it. Two clicks (156 74 twice) before the snap: log
// accumulates "clickedclicked", proving the constructed handle persists
// across events rather than merely surviving one dereference by chance.

window LogWin {
    title: "Log"
    size: 300, 200

    button Add    { at: 20, 20; caption: "Add"; width: 80 }
    textview Body { at: 20, bottom; fill: both }

    var log: text
}

on App.launch {
    open LogWin
}

extend LogWin {
    on Add.click {
        log.append("clicked")
        Body.text = log
    }
}
